      "text_preview": " \nModelling Two Decades of Change and Future Developments \nin the Gulf of Nicoya (Costa Rica) Ecosystem  \nUsing the Ecopath with Ecosim Toolbox \n \n \n \n \nDissertation submitted by \nViola Alms \nto the Faculty 02: Biology/Chemistry (FB02) at the University of Bremen, in \npartial fulfilment of the requirements for the degree of Doctor rerum naturalium \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nSubmission Date: 19th December 2024, Bremen. \n \n  \n\n  \nAdvisory Committee: \n1. Reviewer: Prof. Dr. Matthias Wolff \nProfessor for Marine Synecology / Fisheries Biology, University of Bremen, Bremen, \nGermany.  \nIntegrated Modelling, WG Resource Management, Leibniz Centre for Tropical Marine \nResearch (ZMT), Bremen, Germany. \n \n2. Reviewer: Dr. Marcela C. Nascimento \nEcosystem Modelling Working Group, Research Unit: Marine Evolutionary Ecology, \nGEOMAR Helmholtz Centre for Ocean Research Kiel, Kiel, Germany \n \n \nMembers of the committee: \n1. Examiner: Prof. Dr. Martin Zimmer \nProfessor for Mangrove Ecology (University of Bremen), University of Bremen, \nBremen, Germany \nWorking Group Leader, WG Mangrove Ecology, Leibniz Center for Tropical Marine \nResearch (ZMT), Bremen Germany \n \n2. Examiner: Prof. Dr. Martin Diekmann \nWorking Group Leader, WG Vegetation Ecology & Conservation Biology, University \nof Bremen, Bremen, Germany \n \nAttending students: \n1. Selma Mezger \n PhD-Student (Marine Ecology), University of Bremen, Bremen, Germany \n2. Siri Vietzen \n Student, University of Bremen, Bremen, Germany \nPhD defense: February 10 th 2025, Big Seminar room, Leibniz Centre for Tropical Marine \nResearch, Bremen, Germany  \n \ni \nABSTRACT \nThe Gulf of Nicoya is a highly productive estuary located on the Pacific coast of Costa Rica \nand accounts for the country’s primary fish production. It is increasingly impacted by its ",
